end": 170, "text": "Johnsbury, Vermont on July 15, 1875, the son of Worcester C."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0137-10a83e10786d:d01", "start": 171, "end": 204, "text": "Howe and Rosaline (Bradley) Howe."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0137-10a83e10786d:d01", "start": 205, "end": 278, "text": "He was educated in Caledonia County, and graduated from Lyndon Institute."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0137-10a83e10786d:d01", "start": 279, "end": 401, "text": "Originally trained as a harness maker, he contracted polio which left him unable to perform such demanding physical labor."}, "D2S7": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0137-10a83e10786d:d01", "start": 402, "end": 528, "text": "He began to study law with an attorney in Lyndonville, Vermont, and afterwards attended the University of Michigan Law School."}, "D2S8": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0137-10a83e10786d:d01", "start": 529, "end": 663, "text": "Unable to continue his studies after the first year because of financial constraints, he returned to Vermont and began practicing law."}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0137-10a83e10786d:d02", "start": 0, "end": 131, "text": "James Henry Howe James Henry Howe (December 5, 1827 – January 4, 1893) was known for his position as a United States federal judge."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0137-10a83e10786d:d02", "start": 132, "end": 220, "text": "Born in Turner, Maine, Howe began to study law in 1848 with his uncle Timothy Otis Howe."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0137-10a83e10786d:d02", "start": 221, "end": 361, "text": "During his lifetime, he operated a private law firm, served in the army as a Colonel, and was appointed to be a United States federal judge."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0137-10a83e10786d:d02", "start": 362, "end": 538, "text": "He was the Attorney General of Wisconsin from 1860 to 1862